"""In-depth material for the DBMS lessons."""
from learn import deepen

deepen("dbms-er-relational",
       proofs=[
           ("Number of superkeys with one candidate key, and why every key is a superkey",
            """A superkey is any attribute set whose closure is the whole schema. If the only candidate key is K with |K| = k out of n attributes, a set is a superkey exactly when it contains K: it must contain the k key attributes and may contain any subset of the other n − k, giving **2^(n−k)** superkeys. (If a set contains no complete candidate key it cannot determine every attribute — otherwise a minimal subset of it would be a smaller candidate key.) With several candidate keys count the union of the 'contains Kᵢ' families by inclusion–exclusion: for keys {A} and {B} among four attributes 2³ + 2³ − 2² = 12."""),
           ("Maximum and minimum relationship instances from cardinality constraints",
            """Between entity sets with m and n instances a relationship set is a subset of the m·n possible pairs. In a **1 : N** relationship (one A may relate to many B but each B to at most one A) every B instance appears in at most one pair, so there are at most n pairs; with **total participation of B** exactly n. In a **1 : 1** relationship every instance appears in at most one pair on both sides, so at most min(m, n). In **M : N** every pair may be present: m·n. These bounds give the formulas used in GATE questions; the minimum number of relationship instances is 0 unless participation is total: if every B instance must take part, at least n instances are needed."""),
           ("Minimum number of tables for an ER diagram",
            """Every strong entity set needs its own table. A weak entity set needs its own table too (its key is the owner's key plus the partial key), and its identifying relationship merges into it. A **1 : N** relationship is represented by adding the key of the 1-side to the N-side table as a foreign key (no new table). A **1 : 1** relationship adds a foreign key to either side. An **M : N** relationship cannot be stored in either entity table without repeating groups, so it needs its own table holding both keys. A **multivalued attribute** also needs its own table (owner key + value), otherwise the relation would violate first normal form. Therefore minimum tables = entity sets + M : N relationships + multivalued attributes (+ any ternary relationship that is not reducible)."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Keys and integrity in detail",
            """A candidate key must satisfy uniqueness and minimality; the primary key is the chosen candidate key and may not be NULL (entity integrity). A foreign key may be NULL unless declared NOT NULL, and a non-NULL value must match an existing key value (referential integrity). Deleting or updating the referenced tuple can be handled by NO ACTION/RESTRICT, CASCADE, SET NULL or SET DEFAULT. A table can have several unique constraints but only one primary key. A relation never has duplicate tuples in the pure relational model, although SQL tables are bags unless a key is declared."""),
           ("ER modelling details",
            """Specialisation/generalisation: **disjoint vs overlapping** and **total vs partial**; mapping options are a table per class (subclass tables hold the superclass key), one table for the whole hierarchy with a type column, or a table only for subclasses. Aggregation lets a relationship participate in another relationship. A ternary relationship cannot in general be decomposed into binary ones without losing information. Cardinality ratios describe the maximum, participation constraints the minimum; the double line denotes total participation and the double rectangle a weak entity."""),
       ])

deepen("dbms-relational-algebra",
       proofs=[
           ("Division expressed with the fundamental operators",
            """Let R(A, B) and S(B). The quotient R ÷ S is the set of A-values a such that (a, b) ∈ R for every b ∈ S. Form all candidate pairs π_A(R) × S. A value a fails exactly when some pair (a, b) of that product is missing from R, i.e. when a appears in π_A((π_A(R) × S) − R). The quotient therefore equals **π_A(R) − π_A((π_A(R) × S) − R)**. When S is empty the product is empty, nothing fails and every A-value of R qualifies, matching the vacuous 'for all' reading."""),
           ("Size bounds of joins and set operations",
            """For |R| = m and |S| = n: R × S has exactly mn tuples. A natural join keeps only matching pairs, so 0 ≤ |R ⋈ S| ≤ mn; if the join attribute is a key of S then each R tuple matches at most one S tuple, so ≤ m, and with a foreign-key constraint (every R tuple matches exactly one) exactly m. Union: max(m, n) ≤ |R ∪ S| ≤ m + n; intersection 0 ≤ |R ∩ S| ≤ min(m, n); difference max(0, m − n) ≤ |R − S| ≤ m. Projection removes duplicates, so 1 ≤ |π(R)| ≤ m for non-empty R, and a selection returns between 0 and m tuples."""),
           ("Equivalence rules for query optimisation",
            """σ_{c₁ ∧ c₂}(R) = σ_{c₁}(σ_{c₂}(R)) (selections cascade and commute); σ_c(R × S) = R ⋈_c S; σ_c(R ⋈ S) = σ_c(R) ⋈ S when c only mentions attributes of R, which is valid because the selection and the join test independent attributes of the tuple; π_L(σ_c(R)) = σ_c(π_L(R)) if c only uses attributes in L; joins are commutative and associative. **Heuristic:** push selections and projections as far down the tree as possible to reduce the size of intermediate relations before the expensive joins; order joins so the smallest intermediate results appear first."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Relational calculus and expressive power",
            """Tuple relational calculus (TRC) expressions {t | P(t)} must be **safe**: they may not produce infinite results, e.g. {t | ¬R(t)} is unsafe. Codd's theorem: safe TRC, safe domain calculus and relational algebra have the same expressive power. Relational algebra cannot express transitive closure (reachability) or aggregates such as COUNT without extensions; SQL adds aggregate functions, grouping, recursion (WITH RECURSIVE) and bag semantics. Joins: theta join (any condition), equi-join, natural join (removes the duplicate join attributes), outer joins (pad unmatched tuples with NULL), semi-join ⋉ and anti-join."""),
           ("Typical algebra queries",
            """'Students who have taken every course': division. 'Students who have taken no course': π_sid(Student) − π_sid(Enroll). 'Names of students in CSE': π_name(σ_dept='CSE'(Student)). 'Employees earning more than someone in the same department': a self join with a rename ρ. Number of tuples after a natural join of relations with no common attribute: the product of sizes. In GATE watch out for set semantics: duplicates vanish after projection."""),
       ])

deepen("dbms-sql-basics",
       proofs=[
           ("Why WHERE cannot use aggregates and HAVING can",
            """SQL is evaluated logically in the order FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY. WHERE filters individual rows before any groups exist, so an aggregate such as COUNT(*) is not defined at that point. HAVING is applied to the groups that GROUP BY has formed, so it can use aggregates. Likewise aliases defined in SELECT cannot be used in WHERE (SELECT has not run yet) but may be used in ORDER BY. A column that appears in SELECT without an aggregate must be one of the grouping columns (or functionally determined by them), because otherwise the value would be ambiguous within a group."""),
           ("Row counts of joins from first principles",
            """A cross join returns |R|·|S| rows. An inner join keeps the pairs satisfying the condition, so for an equality on a key of S and a foreign key of R it returns exactly |R| rows (each R row finds its single partner). A left outer join returns every inner-join row plus one NULL-padded row for each left row without a partner: |inner| + (number of unmatched left rows) ≥ |inner|, and ≥ |R|. A full outer join adds the unmatched rows of both sides. For a many-to-many join on a value that appears a times in R and b times in S the join contributes a·b rows for that value."""),
           ("UNION versus UNION ALL and set operations by multiplicity",
            """Treating a table as a bag, UNION ALL adds multiplicities: a value occurring a times in R and b times in S occurs a + b times. UNION eliminates duplicates (multiplicity 1 if a + b > 0). INTERSECT has multiplicity min(a, b) in the ALL version and 1 in the plain version if both are positive; EXCEPT ALL gives max(a − b, 0), EXCEPT gives 1 if a > 0 and b = 0. Therefore |R UNION ALL S| = |R| + |S| always, and |R UNION S| ≤ |R| + |S|."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Aggregates and grouping reference",
            """COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(col) counts non-NULL values; COUNT(DISTINCT col) distinct non-NULL values; SUM/AVG/MIN/MAX ignore NULLs and return NULL on an empty or all-NULL input (COUNT returns 0). AVG(col) = SUM(col)/COUNT(col), so it differs from SUM/COUNT(*) when NULLs exist. With GROUP BY a, b the number of output rows equals the number of distinct (a, b) combinations (treating NULLs as equal). ORDER BY can use column positions; LIMIT/OFFSET choose the page. IN and BETWEEN are inclusive of the end points; LIKE uses % and _; string comparisons may be case sensitive depending on the collation."""),
           ("DDL, DML and constraints",
            """CREATE TABLE with PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE, NOT NULL, CHECK and FOREIGN KEY ... REFERENCES ... ON DELETE CASCADE; ALTER TABLE adds or drops columns and constraints; DROP removes the table and its data; TRUNCATE empties it quickly and usually cannot be rolled back in some systems; DELETE removes rows (with WHERE) and is logged. A view stores a query, not data; it is updatable when it maps rows one-to-one to a single base table. Indexes speed up reads but slow writes. Triggers fire BEFORE/AFTER insert, update or delete, FOR EACH ROW or per statement."""),
       ])

deepen("dbms-sql-nulls-subqueries",
       proofs=[
           ("Why x NOT IN (… NULL …) never returns a row",
            """x NOT IN (a, b, NULL) is shorthand for x ≠ a AND x ≠ b AND x ≠ NULL. The last conjunct compares with NULL and is UNKNOWN in three-valued logic. A conjunction of any TRUE values with an UNKNOWN is UNKNOWN (and with a FALSE it is FALSE). Since the other conjuncts are TRUE or FALSE, the whole condition is FALSE if x equals a or b, and UNKNOWN otherwise — never TRUE. WHERE keeps only rows whose condition is TRUE, so no row qualifies. NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM T WHERE T.y = x) avoids the problem: the correlated test T.y = x is merely UNKNOWN for NULL rows and such rows simply do not make EXISTS true."""),
           ("Three-valued truth tables",
            """With T, F, U: NOT U = U. AND: F dominates (F AND U = F), T is neutral (T AND U = U), U AND U = U. OR: T dominates (T OR U = T), F is neutral (F OR U = U), U OR U = U. A comparison involving NULL is U, including NULL = NULL. Hence for a nullable column c, `c > 8 OR c <= 8` is U when c is NULL and the row is dropped, `c = c` is not always true, and `NOT (c = 5)` is U for NULL. IS NULL and IS NOT NULL are the only tests that return TRUE/FALSE for NULL."""),
           ("Correlated subquery: the second-highest value in three ways",
            """(1) `SELECT MAX(x) FROM T WHERE x < (SELECT MAX(x) FROM T)`: the inner query finds the maximum, the outer maximum over the rest is the second distinct value. (2) `SELECT x FROM T t1 WHERE 1 = (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT x) FROM T t2 WHERE t2.x > t1.x)`: for each row count how many distinct values are larger; the second-highest has exactly one. (3) `SELECT x FROM T ORDER BY x DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1` is the simplest but returns the same value as the maximum when the maximum is duplicated; use DISTINCT first to avoid this. For the nth highest replace 1 by n − 1."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Subquery kinds and evaluation",
            """Scalar subqueries return a single value (an error if more than one row); IN subqueries test membership; EXISTS tests non-emptiness; ANY/ALL compare against a set (> ALL means greater than the maximum, and > ALL over an empty set is TRUE; = ANY is IN; <> ALL is NOT IN). Correlated subqueries are executed (conceptually) once per outer row; uncorrelated ones once. Derived tables in FROM need an alias; WITH defines a named, possibly recursive, CTE. Division in SQL is written with a double NOT EXISTS or with GROUP BY ... HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT ...) = (SELECT COUNT(*) ...)."""),
           ("Views, triggers and assertions",
            """A view is updatable only under restrictions (single table, no aggregates, no DISTINCT); WITH CHECK OPTION rejects updates that would make a row disappear from the view. Materialised views store results and must be refreshed. A trigger runs automatically on an event; row-level triggers see old and new tuple values; they can enforce complex constraints but may cascade. Referential actions: ON DELETE CASCADE deletes dependent rows; SET NULL sets the foreign key to NULL (allowed only if the column is nullable)."""),
       ])

deepen("dbms-fds-closure",
       proofs=[
           ("Soundness of Armstrong's axioms: transitivity and augmentation",
            """Suppose a relation instance satisfies X → Y and Y → Z. Take two tuples t₁, t₂ with t₁[X] = t₂[X]. By X → Y they agree on Y, then by Y → Z they agree on Z, so X → Z holds (transitivity). For augmentation, if X → Y holds and two tuples agree on XZ, then they agree on X (hence on Y) and on Z, so they agree on YZ: XZ → YZ. Reflexivity is trivial: agreeing on X means agreeing on any subset Y ⊆ X. The derived rules follow: union (X → Y, X → Z give X → XY by augmentation, XY → YZ by augmenting X → Z with Y, and then transitivity gives X → YZ), decomposition (X → YZ and YZ → Y by reflexivity), pseudo-transitivity."""),
           ("The attribute-closure algorithm is correct",
            """Let X⁺ be the output of the algorithm (repeatedly add the right side of any FD whose left side is inside the current set). **Soundness:** every attribute added is determined by X, by induction on the steps using reflexivity/transitivity. **Completeness:** suppose X → A is implied by F but A ∉ X⁺. Build a two-tuple relation in which both tuples agree exactly on X⁺ and differ elsewhere. It satisfies every FD of F: if the left side of an FD is inside X⁺ then (by closure) its right side is inside X⁺ too, so the tuples agree there; if the left side is not inside X⁺ the tuples disagree on it, and the FD is vacuously satisfied. Yet it violates X → A. Hence no such A exists: X → A is implied exactly when A ∈ X⁺."""),
           ("Finding all candidate keys systematically",
            """Attributes that never appear on a right-hand side (call them L) must belong to every key, because nothing else determines them. If L⁺ is the whole schema then L is the unique candidate key. Otherwise, try L ∪ {A} for each remaining attribute, then L ∪ {A, B}, and so on, increasing the size and skipping any set that contains a smaller key already found. Attributes appearing only on right sides are in no key. For R(A, B, C, D) with AB → C, C → D, D → A: the core is B (it appears on no right side), B⁺ = B, so extend: AB, BC and BD each have closure ABCD and are all minimal: candidate keys {AB, BC, BD}."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Minimal cover procedure in detail",
            """1) Replace each FD X → A₁A₂… by X → A₁, X → A₂, … (single attribute right sides). 2) For every FD with several attributes on the left remove an attribute B from the left side if the right side is still in the closure of the reduced left side computed under the full set F (B is extraneous). 3) Delete each FD whose removal does not change the closure of its left side (redundant). The result is equivalent to F. Minimal covers are not unique, and different covers of the same FD set may even have different numbers of FDs, so GATE questions either fix the procedure or ask for a property common to all minimal covers. Two FD sets are equivalent if each FD of one is implied by the other, tested with closures."""),
           ("Checking whether an FD holds in an instance",
            """An FD X → Y is violated by an instance exactly when two tuples agree on X and disagree on Y, so one counterexample disproves it, but no number of examples proves it: it is a property of the schema, not of one instance. A key is always a determinant of every attribute. Trivial FDs (Y ⊆ X) hold in every instance. The number of FDs in the closure F⁺ of a non-trivial set can be exponential, which is why closures of attribute sets, not of FD sets, are computed."""),
       ])

deepen("dbms-normal-forms",
       proofs=[
           ("A relation with two attributes is always in BCNF",
            """Let R(A, B). The non-trivial FDs possible are A → B and B → A. If the only FD is A → B, then A is a key (A⁺ = AB), so the left side is a superkey. If both hold, both A and B are keys. If none holds, there are no non-trivial FDs and BCNF is vacuous. In every case each non-trivial FD has a superkey on its left, which is BCNF. The same argument shows that a relation whose attributes are all prime but has a determinant that is not a key is in 3NF yet may fail BCNF."""),
           ("The lossless-join test for a binary decomposition",
            """Decompose R into R₁ and R₂ with R₁ ∩ R₂ = X. **Claim:** the decomposition is lossless iff X → R₁ or X → R₂ holds (under the FDs of R). (⇐) Suppose X → R₁ − R₂ (so X determines R₁). Take a tuple (r₁, r₂) of the join: r₁ ∈ R₁ and r₂ ∈ R₂ agree on X. R₂ derives from some original tuple t with t[R₂] = r₂ and t[X] = r₂[X] = r₁[X]. There is another original tuple s with s[R₁] = r₁. s and t agree on X, hence on all of R₁ (by X → R₁), so the joined tuple equals t and belongs to R. So every joined tuple is original: R₁ ⋈ R₂ ⊆ R, and ⊇ always holds. (⇒) If neither FD holds, a counter-example instance with two tuples agreeing on X but different on both sides produces spurious tuples."""),
           ("Dependency preservation: the projected-closure test",
            """For each FD X → Y of F, start with Z = X and repeat: for each component Rᵢ, replace Z by Z ∪ ((Z ∩ Rᵢ)⁺ ∩ Rᵢ), where the closure is computed with the full set F. If at the end Y ⊆ Z the FD is preserved: it can be derived from FDs that hold inside single components. If every FD of F passes, the decomposition is dependency preserving. The test is polynomial, unlike computing the full projection of F, which can be exponential. BCNF decomposition is always lossless but may lose dependencies: R(A, B, C) with AB → C and C → B has no dependency-preserving BCNF decomposition."""),
           ("3NF synthesis gives a lossless and dependency-preserving decomposition",
            """Algorithm: compute a minimal cover Fc; for each FD X → A in Fc create a relation XA (combine FDs with the same left side); if no relation contains a candidate key of R, add a relation consisting of one key. **Dependency preservation** is immediate: every FD of Fc lies in some relation. **3NF:** within each relation XA, a violating FD Y → B would have Y not a superkey and B non-prime; with a minimal cover this is impossible. **Lossless:** the relation holding a candidate key guarantees that the key can be extended to all attributes through the chain of joins. Hence 3NF is always achievable with both properties, while BCNF is not."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Quick method to find the highest normal form",
            """Step 1: find all candidate keys and prime attributes. Step 2: if every FD has a superkey on the left it is BCNF. Step 3: otherwise if every violating FD has a prime right side it is 3NF. Step 4: otherwise 2NF if no non-prime attribute depends on a proper subset of a candidate key, else 1NF. Short-cuts: if all attributes are prime the relation is at least 3NF; if every candidate key is a single attribute the relation is at least 2NF; a relation in 3NF with only one candidate key is in BCNF. Compare the FDs against the **closures** of left sides, not only the given FDs."""),
           ("Anomalies and higher normal forms",
            """Update anomaly: changing a fact in one row but not others; insertion anomaly: cannot record a fact without an unrelated one; deletion anomaly: deleting a row loses an unrelated fact. A multivalued dependency X →→ Y means that for each X value the set of Y values is independent of the remaining attributes; storing independent multivalued facts in one table requires a Cartesian product of rows (s × l instead of s + l). 4NF requires every non-trivial MVD to have a superkey on the left; 5NF handles join dependencies; denormalisation is sometimes chosen deliberately for performance."""),
       ])

deepen("dbms-indexing",
       proofs=[
           ("Maximum order of a B+ tree node from the block size",
            """An internal node of order p stores up to p child pointers (size P each) and p − 1 search keys (size K each) and must fit in a block of B bytes: p·P + (p − 1)·K ≤ B, i.e. p ≤ (B + K)/(P + K). With B = 1024, K = 10, P = 6: p ≤ 1034/16 = 64.6, so p = 64. A leaf holds p_leaf (key, record pointer) pairs plus one next-leaf pointer: p_leaf(K + P_r) + P ≤ B, so p_leaf ≤ (B − P)/(K + P_r) = 1018/16 = 63.6, p_leaf = 63. Every non-root node is at least half full: ⌈p/2⌉ children for internal nodes and ⌈(p_leaf)/2⌉ keys for leaves."""),
           ("Height of a B+ tree and the number of keys it can hold",
            """With maximum fan-out p and p_leaf keys per leaf, a tree whose root has up to p children has at most p^(h−1) leaves at height h (levels counted with the leaf level as h), so it holds at most p^(h−1)·p_leaf keys: with p = 64 and p_leaf = 63 a three-level tree holds up to 64²·63 = 258,048 keys. Conversely, with minimum occupancy ⌈p/2⌉ the tree with n keys has height at most 1 + log_{⌈p/2⌉}(n/2). Hence the height is Θ(log_p n); in practice 3–4 levels index millions of records, so a search costs 3–4 block reads."""),
           ("Cost of search with and without an index",
            """For n records of size R in blocks of size B the blocking factor is bf = ⌊B/R⌋ and the file has b = ⌈n/bf⌉ blocks. A linear search of an unordered file reads on average b/2 blocks (b in the worst case). Binary search on a file ordered on the search key costs ⌈log₂ b⌉. A **single-level sparse primary index** has one entry per data block: b entries of size K + P in blocks of bfᵢ = ⌊B/(K + P)⌋, so ⌈b/bfᵢ⌉ index blocks; binary search on the index costs ⌈log₂ ⌈b/bfᵢ⌉⌉ plus 1 data block access. A multilevel index applies the same compression repeatedly until one block remains: levels = ⌈log_{bfᵢ} b⌉, and a lookup costs levels + 1."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Index types compared",
            """**Primary index:** on the ordering key field of an ordered file, sparse (one entry per block). **Clustering index:** on a non-key ordering field, one entry per distinct value. **Secondary index:** on a non-ordering field, dense (one entry per record or per value with block lists); there can be many but only one primary or clustering index per file. A dense index has an entry for every search key value; a sparse one only for some. Updates to an ordered file are expensive; B+ trees keep updates at O(log n) by splitting and merging nodes. Range queries are efficient with B+ trees because leaves are chained; hash indexes only support equality."""),
           ("Insertion, deletion and splitting",
            """Insert into the right leaf; if it overflows split it into two halves and copy the middle key up to the parent (in a B+ tree a copy remains in the leaf; in a B tree the key moves up). A split of the root increases the height by one. Deletion removes the key; if a node falls below minimum occupancy it borrows from a sibling or merges with it, possibly propagating upward. The number of block accesses of an insertion is the height plus the number of splits. Extendible hashing doubles its directory when a bucket whose local depth equals the global depth overflows; linear hashing splits buckets in order without a directory."""),
       ])

deepen("dbms-transactions-serializability",
       proofs=[
           ("A schedule is conflict serializable iff its precedence graph is acyclic",
            """(⇒) If S is conflict equivalent to a serial schedule Sₛ, every edge Tᵢ → Tⱼ of the precedence graph reflects a pair of conflicting operations with Tᵢ's first. Conflicting operations keep their relative order in conflict-equivalent schedules, so in Sₛ the whole of Tᵢ precedes Tⱼ. Hence all edges go forward in the serial order of Sₛ and no cycle can exist. (⇐) If the graph is acyclic, take a topological order T_{π(1)}, …, T_{π(k)}. The serial schedule that runs the transactions in that order orders every pair of conflicting operations the same way as S (each conflict contributes an edge consistent with the topological order). Swapping adjacent non-conflicting operations transforms S into this serial schedule, so S is conflict serializable. The number of equivalent serial schedules is the number of topological orders of the graph."""),
           ("Strict ⊂ cascadeless ⊂ recoverable",
            """**Recoverable:** whenever Tᵢ reads a value written by Tⱼ, Tⱼ commits before Tᵢ commits. **Cascadeless (ACA):** Tᵢ reads only values written by committed transactions. If a schedule is ACA there is never a read of uncommitted data, so Tⱼ is already committed when Tᵢ reads it and therefore commits earlier than Tᵢ: ACA ⇒ recoverable. **Strict:** no data item is read or overwritten until the last transaction that wrote it has committed or aborted; in particular reads are of committed data, so strict ⇒ ACA. The inclusions are proper: w₁(x) r₂(x) c₁ c₂ is recoverable but not ACA (dirty read), and w₁(x) w₂(x) c₁ c₂ is ACA (no reads) but not strict (overwriting uncommitted data). Recoverability and serializability are independent properties."""),
           ("View serializability and blind writes",
            """Schedules are view equivalent if they have the same reads-from relation (each read sees the write of the same transaction) and the same final writes. Every conflict-serializable schedule is view serializable, because swapping non-conflicting operations preserves both. The converse fails only with **blind writes** (a write without a preceding read of the same item in that transaction): w₁(x) w₂(x) w₃(x) with T₃ also writing last is view equivalent to T₁T₂T₃ even though conflicts could form a cycle with other items. In the absence of blind writes view serializable and conflict serializable coincide. Deciding view serializability is NP-complete."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("ACID and isolation anomalies",
            """Atomicity (all or nothing), Consistency (valid state to valid state), Isolation (as if serial), Durability (committed effects survive). Anomalies: **dirty read** (reading uncommitted data), **non-repeatable read** (two reads of the same row differ), **phantom** (a repeated query returns new rows), **lost update** (two read–modify–write cycles interleave). SQL isolation levels: read uncommitted allows dirty reads; read committed forbids them; repeatable read also forbids non-repeatable reads; serializable forbids phantoms as well. Transaction states: active → partially committed → committed, or active/partially committed → failed → aborted."""),
           ("Working with precedence graphs",
            """Add an edge Tᵢ → Tⱼ for every pair of conflicting operations (R–W, W–R, W–W on the same item by different transactions) with Tᵢ's operation first. A cycle of length 2 or more means no serial order exists. Count serial orders by counting linear extensions of the DAG: independent transactions can be permuted freely. Remember that commits and aborts are not conflicting operations; items must be matched exactly (x and y are different items)."""),
       ])

deepen("dbms-concurrency-recovery",
       proofs=[
           ("Two-phase locking guarantees conflict serializability",
            """Suppose a 2PL schedule had a cycle T₁ → T₂ → … → Tₖ → T₁ in its precedence graph. An edge Tᵢ → Tᵢ₊₁ means Tᵢ held a lock on some item and released it before Tᵢ₊₁ acquired a conflicting lock on that item. Let Lᵢ be the **lock point** (the moment of the last lock acquisition) of Tᵢ. Tᵢ released the lock after Lᵢ (it can only release after acquiring all its locks in the growing phase ends) and Tᵢ₊₁ acquired it before Lᵢ₊₁, so Lᵢ < Lᵢ₊₁. Around the cycle L₁ < L₂ < … < Lₖ < L₁, a contradiction. Hence the graph is acyclic, and ordering transactions by lock point gives an equivalent serial schedule. 2PL does not prevent deadlock and may cause cascading aborts; strict 2PL (holding exclusive locks until commit) removes cascading aborts and gives strict schedules."""),
           ("Wait-die and wound-wait are deadlock-free and starvation-free",
            """Each transaction receives a timestamp when it first starts and keeps it after restarts. **Wait-die:** an older requester waits for a younger holder; a younger requester aborts. A cycle of waits would need each transaction to wait for a younger one all the way around, i.e. TS(T₁) < TS(T₂) < … < TS(T₁), impossible. **Wound-wait:** an older requester aborts (wounds) a younger holder; a younger requester waits for an older holder, so waiting always goes towards older transactions — again no cycles. Starvation-freedom: an aborted transaction keeps its timestamp, so it eventually becomes the oldest, and the oldest never aborts in either scheme."""),
           ("Basic timestamp ordering produces serializable schedules in timestamp order",
            """Each item X stores R-TS(X) and W-TS(X), the largest timestamps of transactions that read/wrote it. A read by T is rejected if TS(T) < W-TS(X) (T would read a value from its future); a write is rejected if TS(T) < R-TS(X) or TS(T) < W-TS(X). Every accepted operation therefore respects the timestamp order for every pair of conflicting operations, so every precedence edge goes from a smaller to a larger timestamp, and the graph is acyclic: the schedule is equivalent to the serial schedule in timestamp order. There are no deadlocks, since no waiting occurs, but restarts can cause starvation and cascading rollbacks. The Thomas write rule ignores an outdated write (TS(T) < W-TS(X) but ≥ R-TS(X)) instead of aborting."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Recovery in detail",
            """**Write-ahead logging:** the log record must reach stable storage before the data page it describes (undo information) and all log records of a transaction before its commit (redo information). Buffer policies: *steal* (uncommitted pages may be flushed ⇒ undo needed) and *no-force* (committed pages need not be flushed at commit ⇒ redo needed). Recovery after a crash: **analysis** (find dirty pages and active transactions from the last checkpoint), **redo** (repeat history from the earliest recovery LSN) and **undo** (roll back losers, writing compensation log records). Transactions with a commit record are redone, those without are undone. A checkpoint bounds the log that must be examined."""),
           ("Deadlock handling summary",
            """Prevention: conservative 2PL (lock everything first), ordering of resources, wait-die/wound-wait. Detection: build a wait-for graph and abort a victim on a cycle (choose by cost, age or work done). Timeouts are crude but simple. Locks have modes S and X with compatibility S–S only; intention locks (IS, IX, SIX) allow locking at multiple granularities. Multiversion concurrency control lets readers read a snapshot without blocking writers; snapshot isolation can still allow write skew."""),
       ])
